 # kvconf
 
 Tiny configuration helpers for `key=value` text, the kind found in `.env` files
-and simple service configs. Today it only knows how to render.
+and simple service configs. It knows how to render and how to parse.
 
 ```js
-import { renderKeyValue } from "./src/kv.mjs";
+import { parseKeyValue, renderKeyValue } from "./src/kv.mjs";
 
 renderKeyValue({ host: "localhost", port: "5432" });
 // "host=localhost\nport=5432\n"
+
+parseKeyValue("# database\nhost=localhost\n\nport=5432\n");
+// { host: "localhost", port: "5432" }
 ```
 
+## renderKeyValue(object)
+
 `renderKeyValue` writes one `key=value` line per own property, in insertion
 order, and ends the text with a newline. Values are written as they are; a
 value that is not a string throws a `TypeError`.
+
+## parseKeyValue(text)
+
+`parseKeyValue` is the inverse: it reads one property per `key=value` line, in
+line order, and returns a plain object of strings. Values are taken as they
+are, so `parseKeyValue(renderKeyValue(o))` gives back `o` for any object whose
+keys have no surrounding whitespace and whose values are strings without
+newlines. The rules, one per edge:
+
+- A blank line, or one made only of whitespace, is skipped.
+- A line whose first non-space character is `#` is a comment and is skipped.
+- Only the first `=` splits a line: `x=a=b` gives `{ x: "a=b" }`.
+- Whitespace around the key is trimmed: `  host =x` gives `{ host: "x" }`.
+- Whitespace around the value is kept: `greeting= hi ` gives `{ greeting: " hi " }`.
+- Quotes are part of the value, not stripped: `name="Ada"` gives `{ name: '"Ada"' }`.
+- A `#` after the `=` is part of the value, never a trailing comment: `color=#fff # bg` gives `{ color: "#fff # bg" }`.
+- A duplicate key is allowed and the last one wins: `a=1\na=2` gives `{ a: "2" }`.
+- A line with no `=` throws a `SyntaxError` naming the line number.
+- An empty key (`=value`, or only whitespace before the `=`) throws a `SyntaxError` naming the line number.
+- An empty value is fine: `empty=` gives `{ empty: "" }`.
+- CRLF line endings are accepted: a `\r` before the `\n` is dropped.
+- A final newline is optional; the empty string gives `{}`.
+- Anything other than a string throws a `TypeError`.
